/**
 * @file feature_analysis.dox
 * @brief Theory-native feature analysis overview.
 *
 * @page feature_analysis Feature Analysis System
 *
 * The feature-analysis layer derives theory features from the valid system
 * space of an SPL. Its active semantics are system-first:
 *
 * 1. start from the Boolean base feature set declared by the DSL,
 * 2. enumerate candidate systems,
 * 3. keep exactly the valid systems that satisfy the conjunction of asserted
 *    formulas,
 * 4. derive the theory feature registry over that valid system space,
 * 5. classify each theory feature from its simplified logical term.
 *
 * The theory-native categories are:
 * - `Impossible`
 * - `Core`
 * - `Fundamental`
 * - `Not`
 * - `And`
 * - `Or`
 * - `Complex`
 *
 * @section fa_public_types Public types
 *
 * - `feature_t` stores system-membership information.
 * - `feature_record_t` stores one derived theory feature, including:
 *   - its internal numeric identifier,
 *   - its system-membership vector,
 *   - its raw logical term,
 *   - its simplified logical term,
 *   - its derived category,
 *   - and an optional display name.
 * - `system_space_t` stores the valid, available, and missing system sets.
 * - `system_t` stores the features observed in one concrete system.
 *
 * @section fa_feature_analysis_t feature_analysis_t
 *
 * `feature_analysis_t` is the central analysis object. In the active
 * theory-native path it is constructed with:
 *
 * - the size of the Boolean base feature set,
 * - the valid-system set,
 * - the available-system set,
 * - the missing-system set,
 * - and the declared feature names.
 *
 * @section fa_printing Reporting
 *
 * User-facing reporting should stay aligned with the paper representation. In
 * particular, the CLI should present:
 *
 * - paper-style indices `i`,
 * - paper-style feature labels `t_i`,
 * - system-set representations of features,
 * - characteristic vectors `β(t_i)`,
 * - simplified and raw logical terms `ℓ_T(t_i)`.
 *
 * Internal identifiers may remain implementation-specific as long as the
 * printed output stays paper-aligned.
 */
